          title: 'Sets',
          difficulty: 'beginner',
          explanation: 'A set holds unique unordered values. Duplicates are silently ignored. Operations: union (|), intersection (&), difference (-). Lookup with "in" is O(1).',
          why: 'Sets find duplicates, validate allowed values, and compute overlap between column lists — common tasks in data quality checks.',
          syntax: `seen = set()\nseen.add("a")\nseen.add("a")   # ignored — already there\nprint(seen)     # {"a"}\n\nA = {1, 2, 3}\nB = {2, 3, 4}\nprint(A & B)    # intersection {2, 3}\nprint(A - B)    # difference   {1}`,
          example: `incoming = ["orders", "customers", "products", "orders", "customers"]\nexpected = {"orders", "customers", "products", "shipments"}\n\nactual  = set(incoming)\nmissing = expected - actual\nextra   = actual - expected\n\nprint(f"Received: {sorted(actual)}")\nprint(f"Missing:  {sorted(missing)}")\nprint(f"Duplicate count: {len(incoming) - len(actual)}")`,
          expectedOutput: "Received: ['customers', 'orders', 'products']\nMissing:  ['shipments']\nDuplicate count: 2",
          interview: {
            question: 'How do you efficiently find duplicate IDs in a list of 1 million rows?',
            answer: 'Use a set: seen = set(). For each id, if id in seen: mark as duplicate; else seen.add(id). This is O(n) with O(n) memory — much faster than a nested loop O(n²).',
          },
          practice: 'Given ids = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 1, 5, 3], find the duplicate IDs (those that appear more than once). Print the set of duplicates.',
          hint: 'Use two sets: seen = set() and dupes = set(). For each id: if it is already in seen, add to dupes; else add to seen.',
          solution: `ids  = [1, 2, 3, 2, 4, 1, 5, 3]\nseen = set()\ndupes = set()\n\nfor id in ids:\n    if id in seen:\n        dupes.add(id)\n    else:\n        seen.add(id)\n\nprint(f"Duplicate IDs: {sorted(dupes)}")`,
